No, it means "Father of Dis", that is "of the Underworld". Dis is a
contraction of Dives, which is the Roman translation of Ploutoon[1],
which is another name for Hades, who is the Greek god of the Underworld,
but also a name for the Underworld itself.
One place where one can encounter Dis is in "Dis Manibus" or "D.M.",
which one usually sees on Roman epitaphs and sometimes on later ones.

Richard

[1] Both mean Rich, because as god of the Underworld Hades was obviously
    also god of that which is under the world: precious stones and
    metals.


